All-in), -1 (Fold), 0 (Check), 100 (raise the bet), or any value above 100 but lower than your current chips.].\n', 'reasoning': '**Strategic Showdown: Navigating the River**\n\nOkay, so here\'s the deal. We\'re on the river, the board is looking like Q-Q-6-9-10, and I\'ve got a pair of Tens with a King kicker. Not a bad hand, but far from unbeatable. The opponent raised pre-flop, but has checked every street since. That\'s the key here. It screams weakness. If they had a monster, they\'d likely have bet on the flop or turn to build the pot. Now, I have to figure out what they have and how to exploit it.\n\nMy first thought is, "What\'s their range?" They could have anything from air to a slow-played monster. If they have a Queen, I\'m toast. If they have a straight with a Jack, I\'m behind. But the way they\'ve played, I\'m betting the farm they don\'t have a Queen or a straight. That leaves Ace-high, a small pair, or maybe even a busted draw.\n\nNow, do I bet or check? If I bet, I might get called by worse hands, but I also risk getting check-raised by a better hand, which would be a disaster. On the other hand, if I check, I likely win against the Ace-high and the weak pairs. The problem with the paired board is that it\'s easy to get trapped. The King kicker is pretty irrelevant unless they also have a ten.\n\nThe fact that I\'m in the lead overall does play a role here. While the chips reset each hand, I *could* play conservatively. But with a small pot, I should maximize the expected value in this hand. Checking seems like the most conservative play. But, is that optimal?\n\nLet\'s break it down. If I bet a small amount, I might get called by a 9 or a 6, but those hands are small. I lose to any Queen, any straight, or any overpair. The likely scenarios are that the opponent has a medium strength hand and does not get enough value for their bets. The hands that would call a bet and be beat by me are very limited. The hands that are better than mine would gladly